---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'About';
const description =
  'tinavnourish is a slow recipe journal for cooks who actually read the headnotes.';
---

<BaseLayout {title} {description}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pt-16 pb-24">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-terracotta">About</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 font-serif text-5xl font-semibold leading-[1.05] text-ink">
      Notes from a slow kitchen.
    </h1>

    <div class="prose-recipe mt-10 text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      <p>
        tinavnourish is a recipe journal for cooks who actually read the headnotes.
        No auto-playing videos, no six-paragraph story about a holiday before the
        recipe — just a real, tested method, a headnote that explains the
        interesting bits, and notes on what to change when you don't have the
        "right" pan.
      </p>

      <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Seasonal, plant-forward mains and sides with the occasional sweet.</li>
        <li>Times that are honest — prep time is real prep, not "while the oven heats".</li>
        <li>Mixed ingredient forms. "200g flour" is structured; "flaky sea salt, to taste" is not. The schema handles both.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who writes this</h2>
      <p>
        A small group of home cooks who develop and re-test each recipe at least
        three times before it goes up. Authorship appears on every recipe card and
        on each recipe page.
      </p>

      <h2>The longer plan</h2>
      <p>
        A small print cookbook and a pantry shop are both on the roadmap — the
        scaffold for the shop already lives in this codebase. If you'd like to
        publish a recipe here, or stock a product, the contact page is the door.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
